// The per-OS-thread small-slot cache: the allocation path that does not reach
// `heapLock(g)` at all. Sibling of `./gcheap.bit`, same module (SPEC §17.1) --
// every const and helper it uses (`heapBlock`, `heapLock`, `gcHeapAddr`,
// `wordAt`, the `../alloc` names) is declared in a sibling and needs no import,
// because an import is per-MODULE.
//
// Split out of `./gcheap.bit` under the 800-line E0200 ceiling when #5709 gave
// the cache entry its own live-byte word. NO SEMANTIC REASON, and the split is
// where it is because the subsystem was already a banner-delimited section with
// one entry point into the rest of the file: a filename decides emitted
// DIAGNOSTIC order (SPEC §14.8) and nothing else since #3163 made inlining
// order-independent -- see `bitlang-ws/CLAUDE.md`, "Splitting a file". Verified
// a pure move: the module's declaration set is unchanged and
// `scripts/selfhost-diffruntime.sh` lowers every runtime file identically.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The per-OS-thread small-slot cache (#5436)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE PROCESS-WIDE SPIN LOCK PER ALLOCATION IS WHAT THIS EXISTS TO REMOVE.
// `heapLock(g)` is one address in the GC block, so before this every green
// thread on every OS worker serialized every allocation on it. Measured on
// `pkg/web/bench/apps/bit` at 16 keep-alive connections with only the worker
// count varying: 62,964 req/s at `BIT_WORKERS=1`, 27,303 at 2, 14,833 at 4,
// 7,218 at 8, 4,557 at 18 — every worker ADDED subtracted throughput, and
// `bit_rt_spin_acquire` was 87% of on-CPU samples with `gcHeapAlloc` 33,564 of
// its 33,962 callers. The spin is already the tuned form (#2321, see
// `runtime/spinlock.bit`), so the fix is to stop reaching the lock rather than
// to spin better.
//
// A thread takes one CHAIN of a span's free slots under the lock and hands them
// out one at a time with no lock at all, so the lock is taken once per chain
// instead of once per allocation.
//
// WHAT THE COLLECTOR SEES IS UNCHANGED, and that is the whole correctness
// argument. A cached slot is in exactly the state of a slot just taken off its
// span: it is off its span's chains, its span's `spanLive`/`spanFree`/
// `spanEmpty` moved when it was taken (so `heapCheckSpansLocked`'s `sum(total - free) == spanLive` still
// balances, stress-mode check included), no `setSlotBit` bit is set for it, it
// is on no object list, and `gcOwns` therefore answers false for its address.
// Nothing marks it, nothing sweeps it, and nothing can hand it out twice — it
// is on exactly one list, and that list is one thread's.
//
// LIVE BYTES ARE CHARGED AT HAND-OUT, NOT AT REFILL, and that is load-bearing
// rather than cosmetic. `heapLiveWord` is a sum of REQUEST sizes and a free
// subtracts the request size it was given (`heapGiveSmallLocked`), so charging
// a batch at the size of the request that happened to trigger it would drift
// the live total every time a DIFFERENT request size in the same class popped a
// slot out of it — the classes are size buckets, not exact sizes. The refill
// therefore takes its slots at `size` 0 and the pop adds the caller's own
// `size` — since #5709 to this thread's OWN `acLive` word rather than to the
// shared total, reconciled by `gcAllocCacheFlush` below. `gcLiveBytes` is still
// exactly the number it was before this cache existed at every point a caller
// can observe it — `gcShouldCollect`'s trigger, `_tests_/stress/gcbit`'s
// `sizeRoundTrip` (which compares it across collections) and
// `_tests_/stress/gcslotbitmap`'s post-`gcDeinit` leak read — because the flush
// is what stands in front of each of those reads; `acLive` states the bound on
// what is un-flushed in between.
//
// `heapAllocBytes` — the cumulative, never-decremented `allocbytes=` stat whose
// only reader is `runtime/stw/stwstats.bit`'s `statsReport` — DOES move: it is
// charged where the slot leaves its span, so it now runs ahead of the
// bytes actually handed out by whatever is sitting in caches, bounded by
// `acSpanBytes` per class per thread.
//
// THE ENTRY IS KEYED BY THE OS THREAD, so the pop needs no atomic: no other
// thread can reach it. `gcHeapAlloc` is `@nosplit` and contains no safepoint
// poll, so a green task cannot be descheduled between the load of the head and
// the store of its successor, and so cannot resume on another worker inside
// that window. STANDING OBLIGATION: that rests on this path staying free of
// yields — a safepoint added between `allocCachePop`'s two lines would hand one
// slot to two threads.
//
// A COLLECTION NEEDS NO FLUSH, for the same reason the sweep never sees these
// slots: they are not objects and they are charged no live bytes. What a
// cached slot does cost is that its span cannot go entirely free, so a reclaim
// pass will not release it — bounded by the cap below and given back by
// `allocCacheDrainInto` as soon as the entry is reused for another heap.

// Entries in the table. A power of two, and comfortably above any worker count
// this runtime creates (`maxMutators` is 256, but the pool is core-count-sized);
// a thread that finds every probed entry taken simply allocates through the
// lock, which is exactly today's behaviour.
const acEntries = 64

// Words per entry, and the same figure in bytes. 64 words = 512 bytes = eight
// whole cache lines, so no two threads' entries share one. The entry uses 44 of
// them (3 header words, one head per class, 4 accounting words), so even a
// table base that is only 8-byte aligned leaves 20 dead words between one
// entry's last live word and
// the next entry's first — false sharing is impossible without depending on the
// block's alignment.
const acEntryWords = 64
const acEntryBytes = 512

const acOwner = 0 // `gcThreadToken()` of the owning OS thread, 0 when free
const acHeap = 1  // `*(g + gcHeapAddr)` these slots were taken from, 0 when none
const acLock = 2  // `*(g + gcHeapLockAddr)` for that heap, so a drain can lock it
const acHeads = 3 // `acHeads + idx`: head of class `idx`'s cached chain

// #5472: bytes this thread may still allocate before it must read the SHARED
// collection trigger again. `acHeads + allocNumClasses` = 3 + 37 = 40, the
// first word past the class heads and still inside the same 512-byte entry, so
// it is private to the owning OS thread by exactly the argument the heads are
// (see `acOwner`) and shares no line with another thread's.
const acBudget = 40

// #5709: bytes this thread has popped out of its own cache and not yet added to
// the SHARED `heapLiveWord`. Word 41, so private by exactly the argument
// `acBudget` above is and inside the same 512-byte entry.
//
// WHAT IS OWED IS BOUNDED BY THE BUDGET, not by the cache's capacity, and that
// is why this is sound rather than merely cheap. Every allocation that can
// charge this word charges `acBudget` the same bytes — `../gc/gcalloc.bit`
// calls `gcAllocBudgetSpend` on exactly the allocations `allocCachePop` can
// serve — and `gcAllocBudgetSpend` flushes BEFORE it reads the shared total. So
// the arrears never exceed one budget (`gcBudgetMax`, 64 KiB per thread,
// `./gccollect.bit`) and are zero at the instant any trigger is evaluated.
// Under `BIT_GC=stress` `gcTriggerBudget` answers 0, so the flush runs on every
// allocation and the shared word is never behind at all — which is the state
// every stress oracle that reads `gcLiveBytes` was written against.
const acLive = 41

// #5821: objects this thread has allocated and not yet added to the SHARED
// `gcNumObjectsIdx`, and the collector block (`g`) they are owed to. Words 42
// and 43, private by the argument `acBudget` gives. `acGc` exists because the
// count lives in `g` and the drain (`allocCacheDrainInto`) is reached with no
// `g` in hand; it is bound beside `acHeap` and cleared beside it.
const acObjs = 42
const acGc = 43

// Classes a thread caches: every class of at most `acMaxBytes / 2` bytes, so a
// cached class always yields at least two slots per refill. Above that an
// allocation is rare enough that the lock it takes is not measurable and the
// memory a cache would hold is not.
const acMaxBytes = 4096

// Most bytes ONE refill hands ONE thread: one chain, at most
// `allocSpanChainBytes` of one span (#5831, `../alloc/spanmeta.bit`). The
// refill takes it whole with constant work under the lock
// (`heapTakeBatchLocked`, `../alloc/freelist.bit`), and its slots, bitmap words
// and lines stay with one thread instead of being dealt out to every thread
// that refills (#5827).
//
// THE CAP IS A QUARTER SPAN, CHOSEN BY MEASUREMENT. #5827 measured the run
// taken slot by slot (`bench/cases/allocpar`, 8 mutators, M5 Max): 32 KiB
// 2118M cycles / 12.27 MB, a whole 128 KiB span 1887M / 13.42 MB. #5831
// measured both with the chain taken in O(1); the figures are on that ticket.
// WORST-CASE IDLE FOOTPRINT, per OS thread with an entry: 28 cacheable classes
// (every class of at most `acMaxBytes / 2` bytes) x `acSpanBytes` = 896 KiB.
// `acMaxChain` is the longest chain that allows, `acSpanBytes / 8`, and bounds
// every walk of one.
const acSpanBytes = allocSpanChainBytes
const acMaxChain = allocSpanChainBytes / 8

// Probes before giving up and using the lock. Linear, so a collision costs the
// colliding thread one extra load per allocation and nothing else.
const acProbes = 4

// The table itself. `acEntries * acEntryWords`; an array length must be a
// literal, so the two cannot be written as one.
let allocCacheBlock: [4096]i64

// Nonzero once the heap lock has been found HELD by another thread, which is
// the only condition this cache exists for and the only one in which it pays.
//
// A CACHE THAT IS ALWAYS ON IS A LOSS ON EVERY SINGLE-THREADED PROGRAM, and
// that is measured rather than feared. The per-allocation cost of reaching an
// entry is `gcThreadToken`, a cross-object call on every target since the
// #3420 platform split (`./gcthread.bit` states that cost in full), plus the
// hash and the probe; against an UNCONTENDED `spinAcquire`, which is one
// successful compare-and-exchange, that trade is negative. Medians of 5
// interleaved reps on this host, cache always on vs this tree:
// `bench/cases/alloc` 0.227s -> 0.266s (+17%), `bench/cases/strings` 0.222 ->
// 0.243 (+9%), `bench/cases/json` 0.301 -> 0.319 (+6%). Every one of those
// programs is single-threaded, so every one of them was paying for a lock
// nobody else wanted.
//
// ARMED BY THE EVIDENCE, NOT BY A COUNT OF THREADS. The first gate tried was
// "more than one registered mutator", and it never fired: the runtime's
// default worker pool registers several OS threads before `main` runs, so
// every program in the list above still paid. A failed `spinTryAcquire` is the
// exact event the cache removes, so it is what turns it on.
//
// MONOTONIC AND PROCESS-WIDE: it is never cleared, so a program that contends
// once keeps the cache for the rest of its life. A plain word written with the
// same value by any number of threads — the races are all benign, and a reader
// that is late by a few allocations simply takes the lock a few more times.
let allocCacheArmed: i64 = 0

// Arm the cache without waiting for contention to do it (#5441). ADDITIVE: the
// failed-`spinTryAcquire` arming above is untouched, and this sets the same
// monotonic word to the same value.
//
// IT EXISTS BECAUSE CONTENTION-ONLY ARMING IS ALSO COVERAGE-ONLY-ON-CONTENTION.
// Every GC oracle in `_tests_/` but `_tests_/stress/parallel/` is a single
// mutator, so without a forced arm the gates prove the fallback path and not
// this one. Two callers turn it on: `BIT_GC_ALLOCCACHE=1`
// (`../root/rootconfig.bit`) for a one-off run under any policy, and
// `gcConfigure` whenever its `stress` parameter is true (`./gc.bit`), which
// puts the cache under `BIT_GC=stress` — half of every stress-corpus program
// run — permanently and with no harness change.
//
// `@symbol`-pinned for the #1627 reason the `gcEnv*` readers carry: its
// `rootConfigureFromEnv` caller is small enough to be spliced into `boot`,
// whose module emits `--freestanding` and may not name a mangled callee.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_gc_arm_alloc_cache") fn gcArmAllocCache() {
  allocCacheArmed = 1
}

// Arm on a failed `spinTryAcquire` (`gcHeapAlloc`, `./gcheap.bit`). STORES
// ONLY WHEN THE WORD IS STILL 0 (#5826): every allocation reads it
// (`allocCacheArm`), and a store of the same value still takes the line
// exclusive and invalidates every other core's copy, on most refills of a
// contended heap.
@nosplit fn allocCacheArmOnContention() {
  if (allocCacheArmed == 0) {
    allocCacheArmed = 1
  }
}

// This OS thread's entry address, or 0 when it can have none — before boot, on
// a thread with no thread pointer, or when every probed entry belongs to
// another live thread.
//
// THE READINESS GATE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND ITS ORDER IS NOT EITHER. On x86-64 the
// token is a load through `%fs:0`, which FAULTS on a thread whose FS base has
// not been installed yet; `worldBlockAddr()` answers 0 until `rootInit` has
// done that, exactly as `../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s `currentMutator` documents
// for the same pair of calls in the same order.
@nosplit fn allocCacheEntry(g: *i64): int {
  if (worldBlockAddr() == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let token = gcThreadToken()
  if (token == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  // Thread pointers differ in their middle bits (per-thread TLS blocks are page
  // multiples apart), so the low bits alone would pile every thread into a
  // handful of entries. XOR-folding three shifted copies mixes bits 4..21 down
  // into the index; a multiplicative mix is not available here because an i64
  // multiply of an address overflows, which traps in a debug build (§11.1).
  let home = ((token >> 4) ^ (token >> 10) ^ (token >> 16)) & (acEntries - 1)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `acProbes` iterations.
  while (i < acProbes) {
    let addr = base + ((home + i) & (acEntries - 1)) * acEntryBytes
    let e = wordAt(addr)
    let own = atomicLoad(e + acOwner)
    if (own == token) {
      return allocCacheFor(e, g, addr)
    }
    // Claim a free entry. The exchange is what takes it; a thread that loses
    // the race falls through to the next probe.
    if (own == 0 && atomicCmpxchg(e + acOwner, 0, token)) {
      return allocCacheFor(e, g, addr)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0
}

// Bind entry `e` to `g`'s heap, returning `addr` unchanged so the caller reads
// as one expression. A no-op in the steady state (one load and one compare);
// the drain runs only when a thread that cached slots out of one heap allocates
// out of a DIFFERENT one, which is what `_tests_/stress` programs do when they
// tear one heap down and stand the next one up on the same thread.
@nosplit fn allocCacheFor(e: *i64, g: *i64, addr: int): int {
  let heapAddr = *(g + gcHeapAddr)
  if (*(e + acHeap) != heapAddr) {
    allocCacheDrainInto(e, *(e + acHeap), *(e + acLock))
    *(e + acHeap) = heapAddr
    *(e + acLock) = *(g + gcHeapLockAddr)
    *(e + acGc) = int(g)
  }
  return addr
}

// This OS thread's entry when the per-thread paths are armed, 0 otherwise.
//
// ONE RESOLUTION PER ALLOCATION, and that is why it is here rather than inside
// `gcHeapAlloc` where the arming gate used to live: #5472 gave the entry a
// second consumer — the trigger budget below, charged AFTER the object is
// linked — and resolving it twice would pay `gcThreadToken` and the probe
// twice for one allocation.
//
// It is resolved for EVERY size now, where the cache gate skipped anything
// above `acMaxBytes / 2`. That is deliberate: the budget must be charged for
// every allocation or a stream of large objects would raise the shared live
// total without ever spending the budget that guards the read of it. The only
// consequence is that a thread allocating nothing but large objects claims a
// table entry it holds no slots in, which costs one of 64 entries and nothing
// else (a thread that finds none free allocates through the lock, as today).
@nosplit fn allocCacheArm(g: *i64): int {
  if (allocCacheArmed == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return allocCacheEntry(g)
}

// Add what this thread owes the shared live total and object count, and clear
// both debts. `h` is the heap block those bytes were taken from; the objects
// go to the entry's own `acGc`, the block bound with it. The words are private
// to this OS thread, so the reads and the stores need no atomic; only the
// shared adds do.
@nosplit fn gcAllocCacheFlush(e: *i64, h: *i64) {
  let objs = *(e + acObjs)
  if (objs != 0) {
    *(e + acObjs) = 0
    let _ = atomicAdd(wordAt(*(e + acGc)) + gcNumObjectsIdx, objs)
  }
  let owed = *(e + acLive)
  if (owed == 0) {
    return
  }
  *(e + acLive) = 0
  let _ = atomicAdd(h + heapLiveWord, owed)
}

// #5821: THE OBJECT COUNT IS CHARGED BY `gcAllocBudgetSpend` BELOW for a
// thread with an entry, into its private `acObjs`; a thread with none still
// adds to the shared `gcNumObjectsIdx` directly (`allocObject`,
// `./gcalloc.bit`), which is what every allocation did before. The charge sits
// in the budget spend rather than in a helper of its own because a helper was
// not inlined, and one call per allocation is what the single-mutator path
// cannot afford.
//
// THE SHARED ADD WAS THE WHOLE PARALLEL-ALLOCATION PENALTY. `gcNumObjectsIdx`
// is word 4 of the collector block, on the same 128-byte line as `gcHeapAddr`
// (word 0), which `allocCacheFor` reads on every allocation, and `noteBody`'s
// two bounds (words 15, 16). One `ldaddal` per allocation per thread kept that
// line moving between cores, so every allocation's first read of the block
// missed. `bench/cases/allocpar` (8 mutators, M5 Max): `allocCacheFor` was 53%
// of on-CPU samples, and deleting the add outright took the case from 15.8G to
// 6.2G cycles. With no write on the line between collections, every thread
// keeps a shared copy of it.
//
// WHAT IS OWED IS BOUNDED BY THE BUDGET, as `acLive`'s is: every object charged
// here also spends at least its header's bytes of `acBudget`, and
// `gcAllocBudgetSpend` flushes both words before any trigger read, so the
// object half of `gcShouldCollect` is exact whenever it is asked on this
// thread. OTHER threads' counts are settled by `gcAllocCacheSettle` below at
// every collection start, so mark, sweep and `gcCollectFinish` all read the
// exact population.

// Fold every entry's un-flushed object count into `g` (#5821). CALLED WITH THE
// WORLD STOPPED OR BY THE ONLY MUTATOR: from `gcMarkBegin` (`./gccollect.bit`),
// the first step of every collection, and from `gcDeinit` (`./gc.bit`) before
// its walk. `gcMarkBegin` needs it because `recoverOverflow` bounds its passes
// by `gcNumObjectsIdx` and the sweep decrements that word once per freed
// object, so a count that trailed the population would bound the rescan short
// and could go negative. A plain read-modify-write of another thread's word is
// sound here for `gcAllocBudgetClear`'s reason: every owner is parked outside
// `allocObject`, which has no poll.
@nosplit fn gcAllocCacheSettle(g: *i64) {
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  let gaddr = int(g)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `acEntries` iterations.
  while (i < acEntries) {
    let e = wordAt(base + i * acEntryBytes)
    if (*(e + acGc) == gaddr && *(e + acObjs) != 0) {
      *(g + gcNumObjectsIdx) = *(g + gcNumObjectsIdx) + *(e + acObjs)
      *(e + acObjs) = 0
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Charge `total` bytes to this thread's private allocation budget, and one
// object to its private count (#5821, see above), and answer whether an
// allocation-door poll request is due (#5472).
//
// THE POINT IS THE READ IT REMOVES. `gcShouldCollect` opens with
// `heapLiveBytes`, an `ldar` on `heapLiveWord` — the one word EVERY allocating
// thread also `atomicAdd`s — so asking it on every allocation puts an acquire
// load of a permanently-dirty line on the allocation path of every mutator.
// This charges a word no other thread can touch with a plain load, subtract and
// store, and reaches the shared trigger only when the budget runs out.
//
// NOT A SAMPLING SCHEME: the budget is the distance to the byte trigger
// (`gcTriggerBudget`, ../gc/gccollect.bit), so it expires BEFORE the trigger it
// guards can fire, and it is 0 — consult on every allocation, exactly today's
// behaviour — whenever the trigger is crossed, the collector is off, or stress
// is on. `gcCollectFinish` clears it on every collection so a budget taken
// against an old `gcNextBytes` cannot outlive it.
@nosplit fn gcAllocBudgetSpend(g: *i64, entry: int, total: int): bool {
  let e = wordAt(entry)
  *(e + acObjs) = *(e + acObjs) + 1
  let left = *(e + acBudget) - total
  if (left > 0) {
    *(e + acBudget) = left
    return false
  }
  // BEFORE `gcTriggerBudget`, not after (#5709): its first act is
  // `heapLiveBytes`, the shared word this thread is in arrears on. Settling
  // here is what keeps every trigger read exact.
  gcAllocCacheFlush(e, heapBlock(g))
  let budget = gcTriggerBudget(g)
  *(e + acBudget) = budget
  if (budget > 0) {
    return false
  }
  return gcShouldCollect(g)
}

// Drop every thread's budget, so the next allocation on each re-reads the
// trigger. CALLED FROM `gcCollectFinish` (../gc/gccollect.bit) and nowhere
// else: a collection is the one event that moves `gcNextBytes`, and a budget
// measured against the trigger of the cycle that just ended is exactly the
// stale figure that would let a mutator run past the new one.
//
// A PLAIN STORE INTO ANOTHER THREAD'S WORD IS SOUND HERE. Its caller runs with
// the world stopped (`stwCollect`), so every owner is parked; on the
// single-mutator `gcCollectRoots` path there is no other owner to race. Even
// were one to race, the two outcomes are a lost reset and a lost charge, and
// both cost one extra consult rather than a missed one.
@nosplit fn gcAllocBudgetClear() {
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `acEntries` iterations.
  while (i < acEntries) {
    let e = wordAt(base + i * acEntryBytes)
    *(e + acBudget) = 0
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Give every cached slot back to the heap it was taken from, under that heap's
// own lock. `heapAddr` 0 means the entry holds nothing.
//
// NO RECLAIM PASS IS RUN and none is owed: `heapGiveSmallLocked`'s return says
// only that one would now be warranted, and the next free of that class asks
// the same question again (`gcHeapFreeLocked`). Skipping it here is what lets
// this run without a `g`, which the entry does not keep.
@nosplit fn allocCacheDrainInto(e: *i64, heapAddr: int, lockAddr: int) {
  if (heapAddr == 0) {
    return
  }
  // `>> 2`, not `/ 4` — `heapLock`'s argument (`./gcheap.bit`), same word.
  let n: *i32 = nil
  let lk = n + (lockAddr >> 2)
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let h = wordAt(heapAddr)
  // #5709: these bytes were popped out of THIS heap, so they are settled here,
  // under its own lock, before the entry can be rebound to another one.
  gcAllocCacheFlush(e, h)
  let idx = 0
  // Bounded: `allocNumClasses` iterations outside, `acMaxChain` inside — a
  // chain is only ever one chain of one span (`allocCacheFillLocked`), which
  // holds at most `acSpanBytes / 8` slots.
  while (idx < allocNumClasses) {
    let a = *(e + acHeads + idx)
    let k = 0
    while (a != 0 && k < acMaxChain) {
      let nxt = *wordAt(a)
      // `size` 0: this slot was never handed out, so it was never charged any
      // live bytes — see this section's header.
      let _ = heapGiveSmallLocked(h, a, idx, 0)
      a = nxt
      k = k + 1
    }
    *(e + acHeads + idx) = 0
    idx = idx + 1
  }
  *(e + acHeap) = 0
  *(e + acLock) = 0
  *(e + acGc) = 0
  spinRelease(lk)
}

// Drop — without giving back — every slot cached out of the heap at `heapAddr`.
// `gcDeinit`'s, and only ever correct there: the spans those slots live in are
// about to be unmapped, so handing them to a free list that is itself going
// away would be the one way to make a cached slot outlive its heap.
@nosplit fn allocCacheForget(heapAddr: int) {
  let base = int(ptrOf(allocCacheBlock))
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: `acEntries` iterations, `allocNumClasses` inside.
  while (i < acEntries) {
    let e = wordAt(base + i * acEntryBytes)
    if (*(e + acHeap) == heapAddr) {
      let idx = 0
      while (idx < allocNumClasses) {
        *(e + acHeads + idx) = 0
        idx = idx + 1
      }
      // #5709: dropped, not settled — the live word these bytes are owed to is
      // inside the heap `gcDeinit` is about to unmap. Carrying the debt into
      // the entry's next heap would charge it to a heap that never saw them.
      *(e + acLive) = 0
      *(e + acObjs) = 0
      *(e + acHeap) = 0
      *(e + acLock) = 0
      *(e + acGc) = 0
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Put this thread's cached slots back on `g`'s free lists, leaving the heap in
// the state it would have been in had this cache never existed.
//
// THIS IS WHAT KEEPS THE BITMAP ORACLE EXACT, and it is the only reason it is
// exported. `_tests_/stress/gcslotbitmap`'s `checkSpanBitmap` asserts
// `popcount(#3176 bitmap) == spanTotal - spanFree`, i.e. that every slot off
// the free list is a live object — an identity a slot cache breaks by
// construction, because a cached slot is a third state: off the list and not an
// object. Draining first restores the two-state world the assertion is written
// for, so the assertion stays `==` rather than being widened to `<=`, which
// would have stopped catching the stale bits (#3925, #4058) it exists for.
//
// The drain is not a collection and does not need the world stopped: it touches
// this thread's own entry and the heap lock, both of which an ordinary
// allocation on this thread touches anyway.
export @nosplit fn gcDrainAllocCache(g: *i64) {
  let addr = allocCacheEntry(g)
  if (addr == 0) {
    return
  }
  let e = wordAt(addr)
  allocCacheDrainInto(e, *(e + acHeap), *(e + acLock))
}

// Hand out one cached slot of class `idx`, or 0 when the cache is empty. NO
// LOCK AND NO ATOMIC — see this file's header for why the entry needs neither.
//
// IT NEITHER TAKES THE HEAP BLOCK NOR CHARGES ANY BYTES ANY MORE (#5709). This
// used to end in `atomicAdd(h + heapLiveWord, size)` — the last atomic
// read-modify-write left on a warm cached allocation, and a read-modify-write
// of the one line every other allocating thread is also writing. The charge is
// a plain add into `acLive` now, and it is written at the one call site in
// `gcHeapAlloc` (`./gcheap.bit`) rather than here FOR A MEASURED REASON: this
// function sat at exactly `maxInlineLeafInsts()` = 16
// (`compiler/optinlinecost.bit`), so the three IR instructions a private add
// costs pushed it over and turned the hit path's splice into a real `bl` —
// `objdump -r` went from 0 `ARM64_RELOC_BRANCH26` relocations naming
// `_allocCachePop` to 1. Charging in the caller puts the same three
// instructions in the same place the splice would have put them, and keeps the
// splice. Do not move it back without re-running that relocation count.
@nosplit fn allocCachePop(e: *i64, idx: int): int {
  let head = e + acHeads + idx
  let addr = *head
  if (addr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  *head = *wordAt(addr)
  return addr
}

// Fill class `idx`'s empty chain with one whole chain of the heap's. CALLER
// MUST HOLD `heapLock(g)`, and must already have taken the slot it is about to
// return to its own caller — this only fills the cache, so a class that cannot
// yield a single extra slot simply leaves it empty and the next allocation
// refills through the lock again.
//
// ONE TAKE OF CONSTANT WORK (#5831), where #5448 made it one splice and #5436
// `want` pops: the work done under the lock per refill no longer grows with
// the slots it hands over. `../alloc/freelist.bit`'s `heapTakeBatchLocked`
// carries the figures.
//
// THE REFILL STILL NEVER GROWS THE HEAP: it takes only what the class's spans
// already hold, so it cannot map a span and cannot fail.
//
// A chain that is not empty is left alone: the one caller reaches here only
// after `allocCachePop` found it empty on this thread, and no other thread can
// fill it, so a non-empty one would be a chain this refill must not strand.
@nosplit fn allocCacheFillLocked(g: *i64, e: *i64, idx: int) {
  // #5709: the caller holds `heapLock(g)` and is about to touch the shared heap
  // anyway, so a refill is the cheapest place to settle what the pops since the
  // last one owe.
  gcAllocCacheFlush(e, heapBlock(g))
  let head = e + acHeads + idx
  if (*head != 0) {
    return
  }
  *head = heapTakeBatchLocked(heapBlock(g), idx)
}
